<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-recognition" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Finding the Cat: A First Look at Image Recognition</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In <xref ref="sec-images-as-matrices" text="title" /> we did arithmetic to
      pictures, and in <xref ref="sec-superposition" text="title" /> we added and scaled
      them. This section puts that arithmetic to work on a question of a new kind: given
      a small patch of pixels, decide whether it was cut from the corner of the box where
      the cat's eye is, or from the same corner of the other photograph, where there is
      nothing but wall (<xref ref="fig-recognition-two-photos" text="global" />). The
      machine built here to decide it will not be clever. It will subtract, square, add,
      and compare<mdash />and that will be enough to recognize the eye through camera
      grain that all but buries it.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-recognition-two-photos">
      <caption>
        The two photographs the machine must tell apart, with the cut marked on each.
        The outlined rectangle occupies the same place in both<mdash />columns
        <m>231</m> to <m>291</m> and rows <m>297</m> to <m>377</m> of the
        <m>520 \times 780</m> grid of pixels<mdash />and the faint lines inside it divide
        it into the <m>8 \times 6</m> array of <m>10 \times 10</m> blocks whose averages
        are the entries of the matrix. In <xref ref="fig-recognition-alive" text="global" />
        that rectangle holds the cat's left eye; in
        <xref ref="fig-recognition-dead" text="global" /> the cat has left the corner and
        the very same rectangle holds nothing but wall.
      </caption>
      <sidebyside widths="44% 44%" margins="auto">
        <figure xml:id="fig-recognition-alive">
          <caption>The alive cat: the marked block is <m>A_0</m>, and the eye is in it.</caption>
          <image source="images/cat-alive-patch.png">
            <shortdescription>A grayscale illustration of a cat sitting upright inside a wooden box labelled Schrodinger's Cat, beside a mechanism connected to a container marked with a skull and crossbones; a red rectangle labelled A nought, ruled into a grid of small blocks, outlines the cat's left eye</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-recognition-dead">
          <caption>The dead cat: the same block is <m>D_0</m>, and it is bare wall.</caption>
          <image source="images/cat-dead-patch.png">
            <shortdescription>The same box and mechanism in grayscale, with the cat lying motionless on the floor of the box and the container gone; a red rectangle labelled D nought, in the same position as before and ruled into the same grid, outlines an empty stretch of the back wall</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </sidebyside>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Everything is built from tools already on the table: pictures as vectors and
      averages of pictures from <xref ref="sec-superposition" text="title" />,
      coordinates from <xref ref="sec-coordinates" text="title" />, linear
      transformations from <xref ref="sec-isomorphisms" text="title" />, and one import
      from <xref ref="sec-determinant-volume" text="title" />: the dot product. The
      result is a first step, not a finished subject, and the section ends with the
      failures as well as the successes. The failures are the more instructive half.
    </p>
    <p>
      As in <xref ref="sec-superposition" text="title" />, interactive Sage cells appear
      throughout, the cells on this page share their variables, and each should be
      evaluated as you reach it. The lines marked <c># change me</c> are the ones worth
      experimenting with. Each cell is also described in words, so that the argument can
      be followed with no cell evaluated at all.
    </p>
    <p>
      A handful of symbols carry the whole story, and each is defined where it first
      appears. <xref ref="tab-recognition-notation" text="global" /> collects them in one
      place to return to.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-recognition-notation">
      <title>The symbols of this section.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor">
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell>symbol</cell>
          <cell>meaning</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>A_0</m></cell>
          <cell>the clean cat-corner patch</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>D_0</m></cell>
          <cell>the clean empty-corner patch</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>P, Q</m></cell>
          <cell>the two references the classifier is currently using</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>X</m></cell>
          <cell>an incoming picture, to be classified</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>M = \tfrac{1}{2}(P + Q)</m></cell>
          <cell>the midpoint of the two references</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>W = P - Q</m></cell>
          <cell>the discriminating direction, or weight picture</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>\widehat{W} = W / \| W \|</m></cell>
          <cell>the weight picture scaled to length <m>1</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>\tau = M \cdot W</m></cell>
          <cell>the threshold</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>\overline{X}</m></cell>
          <cell>the average of a pile of photographs<mdash />a prototype</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>G</m></cell>
          <cell>a disturbance added to a picture</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>X_{\parallel}, \; X_{\perp}</m></cell>
          <cell>the parts of <m>X</m> along <m>W</m> and perpendicular to <m>W</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>J</m></cell>
          <cell>the all-ones picture</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>\theta</m></cell>
          <cell>the angle between two pictures</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-recognition-distance">
    <title>The Distance Between Two Pictures</title>

    <p>
      Recognition, as this section practices it, is comparison: the machine remembers
      what each corner looks like and asks which memory the new patch resembles more. So
      the first thing needed is a number that measures how far apart two pictures are.
      The dot product of <xref ref="def-dot-product" text="type-global" /> was stated in
      <m>\R^3</m>, but its formula<mdash />multiply matching coordinates, add<mdash />
      never uses the fact that there are three of them. Word for word, the same recipe
      runs on any number of coordinates, and a picture in <m>\mathbb{M}_{mn}</m>,
      read through its pixel entries, has <m>mn</m> coordinates to offer it.
    </p>

    <p>
      It is worth naming what is being used here. Reading a picture through its pixel
      entries<mdash />listing them in a fixed order, say row by row<mdash />is the
      coordinate map of <xref ref="sec-coordinates" text="title" /> taken with respect to
      the pixel basis <m>\mathcal{E} = \{ E_{ij} \}</m> of
      <xref ref="sec-superposition" text="title" />, and that map is an isomorphism
      <md>
        \mathbb{M}_{mn} \;\longrightarrow\; \R^{mn},
        \qquad
        X \longmapsto [X]_{\mathcal{E}}.
      </md>
      By <xref ref="rem-isomorphic-same" text="type-global" />, then,
      <m>\mathbb{M}_{mn}</m> and <m>\R^{mn}</m> are the same space wearing different
      clothes: a <m>780 \times 520</m> photograph is a vector in <m>\R^{405{,}600}</m>
      that happens to be printed in a rectangle. So the definition that follows invents
      nothing. It is the dot product we already have on <m>\R^{mn}</m>, carried back to
      pictures along that isomorphism<mdash />which is also why every fact proved about
      it in <m>\R^n</m> remains true here.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-picture-distance">
      <title>Dot Product, Length, and Distance for Pictures</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For pictures <m>X = [x_{ij}]</m> and <m>Y = [y_{ij}]</m> in
          <m>\mathbb{M}_{mn}</m>, the <term>dot product</term>, the <term>length</term>,
          and the <term>distance</term> are
          <md>
            X \cdot Y = \sum_{i,j} x_{ij}\, y_{ij},
            \qquad
            \| X \| = \sqrt{X \cdot X},
            \qquad
            \operatorname{dist}(X, Y) = \| X - Y \|.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      Unwinding the last of these gives the comparison rule in its most concrete form,
      the one the machine will actually execute:
      <md>
        \operatorname{dist}(X, Y)
        = \sqrt{ \sum_{i,j} \left( x_{ij} - y_{ij} \right)^2 }.
      </md>
      Subtract the two pictures pixel by pixel, square each difference, add up the
      squares, take the square root. It is the root-sum-square of the disagreements at
      corresponding pixels, and nothing more.
    </p>

    <p>
      Two properties can be read directly off the formula and will be used without
      comment: the dot product is symmetric, <m>X \cdot Y = Y \cdot X</m>, and it is
      linear in each argument, so that expressions like <m>(X + Y) \cdot Z</m> and
      <m>(cX) \cdot Z</m> expand exactly as ordinary products do. One consequence is
      worth displaying now, because it will be used twice:
      <md xml:id="eq-expand-square" number="yes">
        \| X - Y \|^2 = \| X \|^2 - 2\, X \cdot Y + \| Y \|^2.
      </md>
      Two more consequences give the distance its meaning. Since
      <m>\| X - Y \|^2</m> is a sum of squares, it is zero exactly when every term is
      zero: the distance between two pictures is <m>0</m> precisely when they agree at
      every pixel. And <m>\| cX \|^2 = (cX) \cdot (cX) = c^2 \| X \|^2</m>, so scaling a
      picture by <m>c</m> scales its length by <m>|c|</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Try <xref ref="def-picture-distance" text="type-global" /> on a
      <m>2 \times 2</m> example. Although the pictures
      <md>
        U = \begin{bmatrix} 90 \amp 60 \\ 120 \amp 30 \end{bmatrix},
        \qquad
        V = \begin{bmatrix} 86 \amp 63 \\ 120 \amp 30 \end{bmatrix}
      </md>
      are <m>2 \times 2</m> matrices, we can regard them as points in <m>\R^4</m>, with
      one coordinate for each pixel. Their difference is
      <md>
        [U - V]_{\mathcal{E}} = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ -3 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}.
      </md>
      Therefore,
      <md>
        \operatorname{dist}(U, V) = \sqrt{4^2 + (-3)^2 + 0^2 + 0^2} = 5.
      </md>
      Since only two coordinates differ, this is precisely the Pythagorean theorem
      applied to a <m>3</m>-<m>4</m>-<m>5</m> right triangle inside the four-dimensional
      space of <m>2 \times 2</m> pictures.
    </p>

    <p>
      The pictures this section will recognize are the two blocks of
      <xref ref="subsec-superposition-numbers" text="title" />: the patch <m>A_0</m>
      around the alive cat's left eye, and the patch <m>D_0</m> cut from the same corner
      of the dead-cat photograph, where the wall is bare<mdash />the rectangle marked in
      <xref ref="fig-recognition-two-photos" text="global" />. Enter them, together with a
      labelled version of the drawing helper from that section and the distance just
      defined. The cell prints the distance between the two corners and draws them side
      by side, each panel titled. Every later cell on this page depends on the names
      defined here, so evaluate this cell first<mdash />and evaluate it again if you
      reload the page or return after a long pause, since either starts a fresh session
      that has forgotten them.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        A0 = matrix(ZZ, [
        [ 65,  63,  80,  84,  81,  79],
        [ 73,  86,  90,  88,  90,  87],
        [ 81,  89,  86,  90,  93,  86],
        [ 89,  68,  76,  69,  95,  91],
        [103,  91,  78,  87,  91,  91],
        [100,  99, 108,  83,  99,  94],
        [ 90,  97, 104, 100, 102,  86],
        [ 84,  94, 110, 107,  70,  63]])
        D0 = matrix(ZZ, [
        [71, 69, 69, 65, 64, 62],
        [74, 72, 71, 67, 66, 65],
        [77, 74, 73, 70, 68, 66],
        [78, 76, 73, 71, 69, 68],
        [82, 78, 76, 73, 70, 69],
        [82, 80, 77, 75, 72, 71],
        [85, 82, 79, 76, 74, 72],
        [86, 82, 79, 77, 75, 72]])

        def show_pictures(*labelled):
            """Draw pictures in a row, each panel carrying a visible label."""
            plots = [matrix_plot(pic.change_ring(RDF), cmap='gray',
                                 vmin=0, vmax=255, title=label)
                     for label, pic in labelled]
            graphics_array(plots, nrows=1).show(figsize=[3*len(plots), 3])

        def dist(X, Y):
            return sqrt(sum(p^2 for p in (X - Y).list()))

        print("distance between the two corners:", n(dist(A0, D0), digits=4))
        show_pictures(("clean cat corner", A0), ("clean empty corner", D0))
      </input>
    </sage>

    <p>
      The two corners sit <m>\sqrt{15678} \approx 125.2</m> units apart. That one number
      is the yardstick for everything that follows: it is the distance a patch would have
      to travel to go from being one corner to being the other, and every verdict the
      machine renders will amount to asking which side of that journey a patch is on.
    </p>

    <question xml:id="rem-recognition-which-basis">
      <title>Whose Coordinates Does the Distance Use?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The formula in <xref ref="def-picture-distance" text="type-global" /> reads a
          picture through its pixel entries<mdash />its coordinates with respect to the
          pixel basis <m>\mathcal{E}</m> of
          <xref ref="sec-superposition" text="title" />. But
          <xref ref="tab-cat-coordinates" text="global" /> showed the same picture
          wearing different coordinates in a different basis. If we computed the same
          formula from those coordinates instead, would we get the same length?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          No. Inside the plane of cats of
          <xref ref="sec-superposition" text="title" />, the picture <m>A</m> has
          <m>\mathcal{B}</m>-coordinates <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
          whose formula gives <m>1</m>, and <m>\mathcal{C}</m>-coordinates
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>, whose formula gives
          <m>\sqrt{2}</m>; and the <m>405{,}600</m> pixel entries of the photograph
          itself give yet a third, vastly larger number. The formula does not read the
          vector; it reads the list of coordinates it is handed, so the number it returns
          depends on which basis produced that list.<fn>
            Stated carefully, a length comes from a choice of measurement rather than
            from a choice of basis. The formula of
            <xref ref="def-picture-distance" text="type-global" /> is one such choice,
            known as the Frobenius inner product on <m>\mathbb{M}_{mn}</m>; the pixel
            basis is orthonormal for it, and so is any other orthonormal basis, all of
            which return the same length. An arbitrary basis<mdash />such as
            <m>\mathcal{B}</m> or <m>\mathcal{C}</m> above<mdash />does not. Inner
            products and orthonormality belong to the course that follows this one; here
            it is enough to fix one basis and never move.
          </fn>
          Throughout this section that basis is fixed once and for all: the pixel basis,
          one coordinate per pixel, which is also exactly the choice under which
          <xref ref="def-picture-distance" text="type-global" /> agrees with
          <xref ref="def-dot-product" text="type-global" /> in <m>\R^3</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </question>

    <remark xml:id="rem-recognition-transport">
      <title>What Else Rides Across</title>
      <p>
        The dot product is not a special case. Any notion that
        <xref ref="def-dot-product" text="type-global" /> settles by a formula in the
        coordinates of a vector<mdash />not only the dot product, but perpendicularity
        and angle as well<mdash />rides to pictures along the isomorphism of
        <xref ref="rem-isomorphic-same" text="type-global" /> in exactly the way
        <xref ref="def-picture-distance" text="type-global" /> has just carried it: read
        the picture through its pixel entries and run the formula. This section will cash
        that promise twice more, once for perpendicular pictures in
        <xref ref="subsec-recognition-perpendicular" text="title" /> and once for the
        angle between two pictures in
        <xref ref="subsec-recognition-limits" text="title" />.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-recognition-nearest">
    <title>Recognition by Nearest Reference</title>

    <p>
      The machine can now be stated in one sentence.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-nearest-reference">
      <title>The Nearest-Reference Rule</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Fix <term>reference pictures</term> <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m>, each carrying a
          label. Given a new picture <m>X</m>, compute
          <m>\operatorname{dist}(X, P)</m> and <m>\operatorname{dist}(X, Q)</m>, and
          assign <m>X</m> the label of the nearer reference. If the two distances are
          equal, the rule declines to answer.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      Take <m>P = A_0</m> with the label <em>cat</em> and <m>Q = D_0</m> with the label
      <em>empty wall</em>, and test the rule first on pictures whose right answer we can
      feel: the blends <m>S_{\lambda} = (1 - \lambda) A_0 + \lambda D_0</m> of
      <xref ref="sec-superposition" text="title" />. No computer is needed, because both
      distances collapse by algebra. Since
      <m>S_{\lambda} - A_0 = \lambda (D_0 - A_0)</m> and
      <m>S_{\lambda} - D_0 = (1 - \lambda)(A_0 - D_0)</m>, the scaling property of the
      length gives
      <md>
        \operatorname{dist}(S_{\lambda}, A_0) = \lambda \, \| A_0 - D_0 \|,
        \qquad
        \operatorname{dist}(S_{\lambda}, D_0) = (1 - \lambda) \, \| A_0 - D_0 \|,
      </md>
      for <m>0 \leq \lambda \leq 1</m>. The rule therefore says <em>cat</em> for every
      <m>\lambda \lt \frac{1}{2}</m>, says <em>empty wall</em> for every
      <m>\lambda \gt \frac{1}{2}</m>, and falls exactly silent at
      <m>\lambda = \frac{1}{2}</m>, where the blend really is halfway to being either
      thing. Its first verdicts are the sensible ones.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        @interact
        def classify_blend(lam=slider(0, 1, 0.05, default=0.3, label="lambda")):
            S = (1 - lam)*A0 + lam*D0
            dc, dw = dist(S, A0), dist(S, D0)
            print("distance to the cat corner:  ", n(dc, digits=4))
            print("distance to the empty corner:", n(dw, digits=4))
            if dc &lt; dw:
                print("verdict: cat")
            elif dw &lt; dc:
                print("verdict: empty wall")
            else:
                print("verdict: undecided")
            show_pictures(("blend at lambda = %s" % lam, S))
      </input>
    </sage>

    <p>
      Dragging the slider draws the blend and reports the two distances. Below
      <m>\lambda = 0.5</m> the cat corner is nearer and the verdict is <em>cat</em>;
      above it the verdict flips; at <m>0.5</m> the two distances print the same number
      and the rule declines, exactly as the algebra promised.
    </p>

    <p>
      Blends are a tame test: they live on the line segment joining the two references,
      drawn in coordinates in <xref ref="fig-cats-segment" text="global" />.
      A real second photograph of either corner would not. Sensors add
      <term>grain</term><mdash />each pixel arrives a little brighter or darker than the
      scene, by an amount that differs from shot to shot. Model it in the simplest way:
      each pixel receives its own random error, independent of the others, with mean
      <m>0</m> and standard deviation <m>\sigma</m> gray levels. A new photograph of the
      cat corner is then <m>A_0 + G</m>, where <m>G</m> is a picture of pure noise, and
      since gray levels below <m>0</m> or above <m>255</m> do not exist, the few pixels
      the grain pushes outside that range are clipped back to it. The cell below
      manufactures such photographs, with grain of a size you control, and scores the
      rule on two hundred of them.
    </p>

    <remark xml:id="rem-recognition-probability-signpost">
      <title>Where Probability Is Needed, and Where It Is Not</title>
      <p>
        This section uses probability at two different depths, and it is worth marking
        which is which before the grain arrives. The shallow use is everywhere and asks
        nothing: grain is modelled as an error at each pixel, independent of the others,
        with mean <m>0</m> and standard deviation <m>\sigma</m>, and experiments are
        scored by counting how many verdicts out of a few hundred came out right. Those
        two phrases are the whole of what is needed to read the main line of the section.
        The deep use<mdash />distributions, the normal curve, the law of large numbers,
        the central limit theorem<mdash />is confined to the starred subsection
        <xref ref="subsec-recognition-accuracy" text="title" /> and to two footnotes,
        <xref ref="fn-recognition-root-k" /> and <xref ref="fn-recognition-clt" />, each
        of which names the theorem doing the work rather than assuming it. Nothing later
        in the book depends on any of it.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <sage>
      <input>
        set_random_seed(53)   # change me: any integer gives a different night of photographs
        sigma = 25            # change me: the typical size of the grain, in gray levels

        def grainy(X, sigma):
            noise = matrix(RDF, 8, 6, [normalvariate(0, sigma) for _ in range(48)])
            shot  = X.change_ring(RDF) + noise
            # gray levels live in [0, 255]: clip whatever the grain pushed outside
            return matrix(RDF, 8, 6, [min(255.0, max(0.0, v)) for v in shot.list()])

        trials, correct = 200, 0
        for k in range(trials):
            corner = A0 if k % 2 == 0 else D0
            shot = grainy(corner, sigma)
            verdict = A0 if dist(shot, A0) &lt; dist(shot, D0) else D0
            correct += (verdict is corner)
        print(correct, "correct out of", trials)
        print("typical distance the grain moves a shot:",
              n(sigma*sqrt(48), digits=4))
        show_pictures(("grainy cat photograph", grainy(A0, sigma)),
                      ("clean reference", A0))
      </input>
    </sage>

    <p>
      At <c>sigma = 25</c> the rule is nearly perfect, and that should be more surprising
      than it looks. Grain of that size moves a shot about
      <m>25\sqrt{48} \approx 173</m> units<mdash />farther than the <m>125.2</m> that
      separate the two corners in the first place.<fn>
        The number <m>\sigma\sqrt{48}</m> is the typical size of the displacement, not
        its exact value in every shot: each photograph lands a little nearer or a little
        farther, and only the average behaviour is pinned down by <m>\sigma</m>.
      </fn>
      A typical grainy photograph of the eye ends up farther from <m>A_0</m> than the
      empty corner is, and the rule still sorts them correctly. Why the verdicts survive
      a displacement larger than the gap between the classes is the central fact of the
      whole section, and it comes out of the next subsection in a single line.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-recognition-score">
    <title>The Score and the Decision Boundary</title>

    <p>
      The rule as stated computes two distances and compares them. Expanding both with
      the identity <xref ref="eq-expand-square" /> of
      <xref ref="subsec-recognition-distance" text="title" /> shows that it is secretly
      doing something simpler. The picture <m>X</m> is closer to <m>P</m> than to
      <m>Q</m> exactly when <m>\| X - P \|^2 \lt \| X - Q \|^2</m>, that is, when
      <md>
        \| X \|^2 - 2\, X \cdot P + \| P \|^2
        \lt \| X \|^2 - 2\, X \cdot Q + \| Q \|^2.
      </md>
      The term <m>\| X \|^2</m> cancels from both sides<mdash />the length of the
      incoming picture never mattered at all<mdash />and what remains is
      <m>2\, X \cdot (P - Q) \gt \| P \|^2 - \| Q \|^2</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Two pictures built from the references turn that inequality into geometry. Let
      <md>
        M = \tfrac{1}{2}(P + Q)
        \qquad \text{and} \qquad
        W = P - Q
      </md>
      be the <term>midpoint</term> of the two references and the
      <term>discriminating direction</term> that runs from <m>Q</m> to <m>P</m>. Since
      <m>\| P \|^2 - \| Q \|^2 = (P + Q) \cdot (P - Q) = 2\, M \cdot W</m>, the
      inequality above reads <m>X \cdot W \gt M \cdot W</m>, or, with everything on one
      side,
      <md>
        \operatorname{dist}(X, P) \lt \operatorname{dist}(X, Q)
        \quad \text{if and only if} \quad
        (X - M) \cdot W \gt 0.
      </md>
      Define the <term>score</term> of a picture to be that quantity,
      <md>
        s(X) = (X - M) \cdot W.
      </md>
      The rule says <em>cat</em> when the score is positive, <em>empty wall</em> when it
      is negative, and nothing when it is zero.
    </p>

    <p>
      The score measures the incoming picture from the midpoint <m>M</m>, in the single
      direction <m>W</m> that
      points from the empty-wall reference toward the cat reference. Its sign says which
      side of the midpoint <m>X</m> lies on. The set where <m>s(X) = 0</m><mdash />the
      pictures equidistant from both references, which the rule refuses to
      judge<mdash />is the flat through <m>M</m> perpendicular to <m>W</m>, the
      <term>decision boundary</term> that cuts picture space into a cat side and an
      empty side.
    </p>

    <p>
      Multiplying out <m>(X - M) \cdot W</m> puts the same score in the form a machine
      would store:
      <md>
        s(X) = X \cdot W - \tau,
        \qquad \text{where} \quad
        \tau = M \cdot W = \tfrac{1}{2}\left( \| P \|^2 - \| Q \|^2 \right).
      </md>
      Everything the machine will ever decide is contained in one picture and one
      number: the <term>weight picture</term> <m>W</m><fn>
        The name comes from the role the entries of <m>W</m> play in the sum. Written
        out, the score is
        <m>X \cdot W - \tau = \sum_{i,j} x_{ij}\, w_{ij} - \tau</m>, so each entry
        <m>w_{ij}</m> is the <em>weight</em> attached to pixel <m>(i,j)</m>: how much
        that pixel's brightness counts toward the verdict, and in which
        direction<mdash />positive entries vote <em>cat</em>, negative ones vote
        <em>empty wall</em>, and near-zero entries are ignored.
      </fn> and the <term>threshold</term> <m>\tau</m>. The two references themselves score
      <md>
        s(P) = \tfrac{1}{2}\, \| W \|^2,
        \qquad
        s(Q) = -\tfrac{1}{2}\, \| W \|^2,
      </md>
      placed symmetrically on either side of the boundary, each at the same distance from
      it. They are not the ends of the scale, however: a picture farther out along
      <m>W</m> than <m>P</m> scores higher than <m>P</m> does, and scores lie anywhere on
      the number line. For our references <m>P = A_0</m> and <m>Q = D_0</m> the numbers
      are concrete. The squared lengths are <m>\| A_0 \|^2 = 375632</m> and
      <m>\| D_0 \|^2 = 259930</m>, so
      <m>\tau = (375632 - 259930)/2 = 57851</m>, and
      <m>s(A_0) = +7839</m> while <m>s(D_0) = -7839</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The map <m>T(X) = X \cdot W</m> is a linear transformation from
      <m>\mathbb{M}_{8,6}</m> to <m>\R</m> in the sense of
      <xref ref="def-general-linear-transformation" text="type-global" />, because the
      dot product is linear in its first argument; the score differs from it only by the
      constant <m>\tau</m>.<fn>
        Only <m>T</m> is a linear transformation. The score <m>s(X) = T(X) - \tau</m> is
        not one unless <m>\tau = 0</m>, since a linear transformation must send the zero
        picture to <m>0</m> and <m>s(0) = -\tau</m>. A linear transformation followed by
        the addition of a constant is called <em>affine</em>. The distinction costs
        nothing below, because every claim made about how the score
        <em>changes</em> is a claim about <m>T</m>, and the constant cancels out of
        differences.
      </fn>
      Linearity is not a spectator here<mdash />it is why the blends of the previous
      subsection behaved so cleanly: along the segment from <m>A_0</m> to <m>D_0</m> the
      score varies linearly with <m>\lambda</m>,
      <m>s(S_\lambda) = 7839\,(1 - 2\lambda)</m>, sliding at constant speed from
      <m>+7839</m> to <m>-7839</m> and crossing zero at exactly
      <m>\lambda = \frac{1}{2}</m>.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-weight-picture">
      <title>Every Linear Verdict Is a Weight Picture</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T \colon \mathbb{M}_{8,6} \to \R</m> be any linear transformation.
          Then there is exactly one picture <m>W</m> with <m>T(X) = X \cdot W</m> for
          every <m>X</m>, namely the picture whose <m>(i,j)</m> entry is
          <m>T(E_{ij})</m>, where <m>\mathcal{E} = \{ E_{ij} \}</m> is the pixel basis.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Every picture is a linear combination of the pixel basis,
          <m>X = \sum_{i,j} x_{ij} E_{ij}</m>, so linearity of <m>T</m> gives
          <md>
            T(X) = \sum_{i,j} x_{ij}\, T(E_{ij}).
          </md>
          Let <m>W</m> be the picture with entries <m>w_{ij} = T(E_{ij})</m>. The sum on
          the right is then <m>\sum_{i,j} x_{ij} w_{ij}</m>, which is <m>X \cdot W</m>
          by <xref ref="def-picture-distance" text="type-global" />. For uniqueness,
          suppose <m>T(X) = X \cdot V</m> for every <m>X</m>. Feeding the machine a
          single lit pixel, <m>X = E_{ij}</m>, gives
          <m>v_{ij} = E_{ij} \cdot V = T(E_{ij}) = w_{ij}</m>, and this at every
          <m>(i,j)</m> says <m>V = W</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>

    <p>
      So the weight picture is not an accident of the <m>P - Q</m> construction. Every
      one-neuron verdict that is linear in the incoming picture is a dot product against
      some picture, however its weights were arrived at<mdash />by subtracting two
      references, by training on a million examples, or by guessing<mdash />and the
      theorem even says how to extract that picture from a machine that will not say what
      is inside it: light up one pixel at a time and record what it reports. The
      <m>1 \times 48</m> matrix of <m>T</m> in the pixel basis, written down in the next
      paragraph, is exactly the entries of <m>W</m> laid out in a row.
    </p>

    <p>
      As in <xref ref="sec-matrix-of-transformation" text="title" />, the transformation
      <m>T</m> has a matrix with respect to the pixel basis: the <m>1 \times 48</m> row
      that lists the <m>48</m> weights. Because <m>W \neq 0</m>, that row is not the zero
      row, so <m>T</m> has rank <m>1</m>, and
      <xref ref="thm-rank-nullity" text="type-global" /> gives
      <m>\dim \ker(T) = 48 - 1 = 47</m>. The kernel is where the machine's blindness
      lives. If <m>G</m> lies in it, then adding <m>G</m> to any picture leaves the
      verdict untouched:
      <md>
        s(X + G) = s(X) + G \cdot W = s(X).
      </md>
      Note what this does and does not say. A picture <m>G</m> in <m>\ker(T)</m> need not
      have score zero itself; what is zero is the change it causes. So <m>\ker(T)</m> is
      the <m>47</m>-dimensional space of directions of change the classifier cannot
      detect, and recognition, for this machine, is one linear transformation followed by
      one comparison.
    </p>

    <remark xml:id="rem-recognition-level-sets">
      <title>The Boundary Comes in Parallel Copies</title>
      <p>
        The boundary is one member of a family. Fix a number <m>c</m> and a picture
        <m>X_0</m> with <m>s(X_0) = c</m>. Then <m>s(X) = c</m> exactly when
        <m>s(X) - s(X_0) = (X - X_0) \cdot W = 0</m>, that is, exactly when
        <m>X - X_0</m> lies in <m>\ker(T)</m>. So each set
        <m>\{ X : s(X) = c \}</m> is the translate <m>X_0 + \ker(T)</m><mdash />a
        parallel copy of the boundary <m>\{ s = 0 \}</m>, one copy for every real
        number, stacked through picture space like the pages of a book. The blend line
        <m>S_{\lambda}</m> cuts through them at constant speed, and that is the whole
        geometric content of <m>s(S_{\lambda}) = 7839\,(1 - 2\lambda)</m>. The picture
        <m>W</m> stands perpendicular to every page, in the sense
        <xref ref="subsec-recognition-perpendicular" text="title" /> makes precise.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <p>
      The weight picture itself is worth looking at, and it is an old friend: for these
      references, <m>W = A_0 - D_0</m> is exactly twice the difference picture
      <m>\Delta</m> of <xref ref="sec-superposition" text="title" />. Its entries are
      near zero along the wall and the lighting that the two corners share, and largest
      in size at the eye, where they disagree (compare
      <xref ref="fig-cat-difference" text="global" />). The machine was never told where
      to look. Subtracting the two references built its attention for it: shared
      background cancels, and whatever remains is precisely what distinguishes the
      classes. The cell below draws <m>W</m>, shifted to mid-gray for display exactly as
      <m>\Delta</m> was, and confirms the scores.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        W   = A0 - D0
        M   = (A0 + D0)/2
        tau = sum(m*w for m, w in zip(M.list(), W.list()))   # tau = M . W

        def score(X):
            return sum(x*w for x, w in zip(X.list(), W.list())) - tau

        print("tau =", tau)
        print("score of the cat corner:  ", score(A0))
        print("score of the empty corner:", score(D0))
        print("score of the midpoint:    ", score(M))
        lam = 0.3   # change me
        print("score of the blend:", n(score((1 - lam)*A0 + lam*D0), digits=5),
              "  predicted 7839*(1 - 2*lambda) =", n(7839*(1 - 2*lam), digits=5))

        J = ones_matrix(ZZ, 8, 6)
        show_pictures(("weight picture W, mid-gray = 0", 128*J + W))
      </input>
    </sage>

    <p>
      The cell prints <m>\tau = 57851</m>, the symmetric scores <m>\pm 7839</m>, a score
      of exactly <m>0</m> at the midpoint, and a blend score matching the linear
      prediction. The drawing shows <m>W</m> against a mid-gray background standing for
      zero: featureless where the two corners agree, and bright and dark only where the
      eye is.
    </p>

    <p>
      Read for an arbitrary <m>G</m> rather than one in the kernel, that same line
      settles the surprise left hanging by the grain. For <em>any</em> disturbance
      <m>G</m> whatever,
      <md>
        s(X + G) = s(X) + G \cdot W,
      </md>
      so the verdict on <m>X + G</m> differs from the verdict on <m>X</m> only through
      the single number <m>G \cdot W</m><mdash />never through the size of <m>G</m>.
      Grain is a picture whose <m>48</m> entries have no relationship to <m>W</m>: in the
      sum <m>G \cdot W</m> the positive and negative contributions arrive in comparable
      amounts and largely cancel, so grain can move a shot <m>173</m> units while barely
      touching the score. The distance saw an enormous displacement; the score, which is
      all that decides, saw almost none. A far shorter disturbance aimed squarely along
      <m>W</m> would do what the grain could not.
      <xref ref="fig-decision-boundary" text="global" /> is the whole story in two
      dimensions.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-decision-boundary">
      <caption>
        What the classifier actually watches. The boundary <m>s(X) = 0</m> is the flat
        through the midpoint <m>M</m> perpendicular to <m>W = P - Q</m>. A long
        disturbance <m>G</m> that runs nearly along the boundary changes the score
        hardly at all; a much shorter one aimed along <m>W</m> carries the picture across
        it. Only the part of the movement in the <m>W</m>-direction counts.
      </caption>
      <image width="90%">
        <shortdescription>A two-dimensional schematic: a vertical decision boundary line with the empty-wall region on the left and the cat region on the right; the references Q and P sit symmetrically on either side with their midpoint M on the line; an arrow labelled W runs from Q to P perpendicular to the line; a long arrow from P runs nearly parallel to the boundary and stays on the cat side; a shorter arrow from P aimed back along W crosses the boundary into the empty-wall side</shortdescription>
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.1, every node/.style={font=\small}]
            \fill[red!7]  (-4.2,-1.8) rectangle (0,3.2);
            \fill[blue!7] (0,-1.8) rectangle (5.4,3.2);
            \draw[very thick, black!70] (0,-1.8) -- (0,3.2);
            \node[black!70, anchor=west] at (0.12,1.5) {$s(X)=0$};
            \node[anchor=north east, align=right] at (-0.2,3.15)
              {$s(X)$ negative:\\ \emph{empty wall}};
            \node[anchor=north west, align=left] at (0.2,3.15)
              {$s(X)$ positive:\\ \emph{cat}};
            \draw[dashed, black!55] (-1.2,0) -- (1.2,0);
            \fill (1.2,0) circle (1.7pt) node[below right=-1pt] {$P$};
            \fill (-1.2,0) circle (1.7pt) node[above left=-2pt] {$Q$};
            \fill (0,0) circle (1.7pt) node[above left=-2pt] {$M$};
            \draw[violet, very thick, -{Stealth[length=2.2mm]}] (-1.2,-1.15) -- (1.2,-1.15);
            \node[anchor=east] at (-1.32,-1.15) {$W = P - Q$};
            \draw[green!50!black, very thick, -{Stealth[length=2.2mm]}] (1.2,0) -- (1.85,2.5);
            \node[anchor=west, align=left] at (1.98,2.3)
              {grain $G$: long,\\ but $G \cdot W$ small};
            \draw[red!80!black, very thick, -{Stealth[length=2.2mm]}] (1.2,0) -- (-0.62,-0.45);
            \node[anchor=east, align=right] at (-0.74,-0.45)
              {dimming: short,\\ but aimed along $W$};
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <remark xml:id="rem-recognition-neuron">
      <title>One Neuron</title>
      <p>
        An object that multiplies its inputs by fixed weights, adds, subtracts a
        threshold, and reports only the sign has a name: it is an
        <term>artificial neuron</term>, and a machine made of exactly one of them is the
        <term>perceptron</term>, proposed in 1958 as a model of how a nerve cell might
        decide.<fn>
          Frank Rosenblatt built the first one at the Cornell Aeronautical Laboratory;
          its intended application was, from the beginning, recognizing images.
        </fn>
        A modern image recognizer is millions of these composed in layers, with two
        upgrades: the weights are not obtained by averaging two references but adjusted
        step by step to reduce error on examples, and a simple nonlinear function is
        applied between layers<mdash />necessarily, since applying one linear
        transformation after another yields a transformation that is again linear, so
        that without the nonlinearity a whole stack of layers could do no more than one
        neuron pretending to be many. The scale is new; the arithmetic inside is the
        arithmetic of this section.
      </p>
      <p>
        The blindness scales too. A layer of <m>k</m> neurons is a linear transformation
        <m>\mathbb{M}_{8,6} \to \R^k</m> followed by <m>k</m> thresholds, and applying
        <xref ref="thm-weight-picture" text="type-global" /> to each of its <m>k</m>
        outputs shows that its matrix in the pixel basis has the <m>k</m> weight pictures
        as its rows. <xref ref="thm-rank-nullity" text="type-global" /> then forces the
        kernel of that transformation to have dimension at least <m>48 - k</m>: no matter
        how the weights are trained, at least <m>48 - k</m> dimensions of change stay
        invisible to the entire layer, and
        <xref ref="subsec-recognition-perpendicular" text="title" /> will identify them
        as the pictures perpendicular to all <m>k</m> weight pictures at once.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-recognition-perpendicular">
    <title>Perpendicular Pictures and What the Score Ignores</title>

    <p>
      Two facts from the last subsection were stated and left standing. The kernel of
      <m>T</m> has dimension <m>47</m>, a number with no meaning attached to it yet. And
      grain that moves a shot <m>173</m> units barely disturbs a score whose whole scale
      is <m>7839</m>. One idea settles both, and it is the one promised in
      <xref ref="rem-recognition-transport" text="type-global" />.
    </p>

    <p>
      In <m>\R^3</m>, <xref ref="def-dot-product" text="type-global" /> fixes the angle
      between two nonzero vectors by
      <m>\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v} = \| \vec{u} \| \| \vec{v} \| \cos\theta</m>, so
      <m>\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v} = 0</m> says <m>\cos\theta = 0</m>: the two vectors meet
      at a right angle. That condition is a statement about a number computed from
      coordinates, and nothing else, so it rides to pictures exactly as the dot product
      itself did.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-picture-perpendicular">
      <title>Perpendicular Pictures</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Two pictures <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m> in <m>\mathbb{M}_{mn}</m> are
          <term>perpendicular</term> when
          <md>
            X \cdot Y = \sum_{i,j} x_{ij} \, y_{ij} = 0.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      The zero picture is perpendicular to everything, and no nonzero picture is
      perpendicular to itself, since <m>X \cdot X = \| X \|^2</m>. Two consequences
      follow immediately, and both will be used.
    </p>

    <remark xml:id="rem-picture-pythagoras">
      <title>Pythagoras for Pictures</title>
      <p>
        If <m>A \cdot B = 0</m>, then
        <md>
          \| A + B \|^2 = \| A \|^2 + \| B \|^2.
        </md>
        Read <xref ref="eq-expand-square" /> with <m>-B</m> in place of <m>Y</m>: it
        becomes <m>\| A + B \|^2 = \| A \|^2 + 2\, A \cdot B + \| B \|^2</m>, and the
        middle term is zero by assumption. The <m>3</m>-<m>4</m>-<m>5</m> triangle of
        <xref ref="subsec-recognition-distance" text="title" /> was this identity in the
        <m>2 \times 2</m> case.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <lemma xml:id="lem-picture-dot-bound">
      <title>The Dot Product Cannot Outrun the Lengths</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For all pictures <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m> in <m>\mathbb{M}_{mn}</m>,
          <md>
            | X \cdot Y | \leq \| X \| \, \| Y \|,
          </md>
          with equality exactly when one of the two pictures is a scalar multiple of the
          other.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          If <m>Y</m> is the zero picture both sides are <m>0</m> and <m>Y = 0 \cdot X</m>,
          so assume <m>Y \neq 0</m> and set
          <m>Z = \| Y \|^2 X - (X \cdot Y) Y</m>. Expanding <m>\| Z \|^2 = Z \cdot Z</m>
          by linearity in each argument,
          <md>
            \| Z \|^2
            = \| Y \|^4 \| X \|^2 - 2 \| Y \|^2 (X \cdot Y)^2 + (X \cdot Y)^2 \| Y \|^2
            = \| Y \|^2 \left( \| X \|^2 \| Y \|^2 - (X \cdot Y)^2 \right).
          </md>
          The left side is a sum of squares, hence <m>\geq 0</m>, and
          <m>\| Y \|^2 \gt 0</m>, so <m>(X \cdot Y)^2 \leq \| X \|^2 \| Y \|^2</m>.
          Taking square roots gives the inequality. Equality holds exactly when
          <m>\| Z \|^2 = 0</m>, that is, when <m>Z</m> is the zero picture, which says
          <m>X = \frac{X \cdot Y}{\| Y \|^2} \, Y</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </lemma>

    <p>
      The inequality is an old one<mdash />it carries the names of Cauchy and
      Schwarz<mdash />and it is the statement that a dot product is largest, for given
      lengths, when the two pictures point the same way, and shrinks to nothing when they
      are perpendicular. Everything in the rest of this subsection is that sentence made
      arithmetic.
    </p>

    <p>
      Now specialize to the one direction the classifier watches. Since
      <m>\| cX \| = |c| \, \| X \|</m>, the picture
      <md>
        \widehat{W} = \frac{W}{\| W \|}
      </md>
      is <m>W</m> scaled to length <m>1</m>: same direction, unit length,
      <m>\widehat{W} \cdot \widehat{W} = 1</m>. For our references <m>\| W \| = \sqrt{15678} \approx 125.2</m>, so
      <m>\widehat{W}</m> is the weight picture with every entry divided by that. Given
      any picture <m>X</m>, put
      <md>
        X_{\parallel} = (X \cdot \widehat{W}) \, \widehat{W},
        \qquad
        X_{\perp} = X - X_{\parallel},
      </md>
      so that <m>X = X_{\parallel} + X_{\perp}</m> by construction. The two pieces are
      perpendicular, by a computation with no idea in it:
      <md>
        X_{\parallel} \cdot X_{\perp}
        = (X \cdot \widehat{W}) \, (\widehat{W} \cdot X)
          - (X \cdot \widehat{W})^2 \, (\widehat{W} \cdot \widehat{W})
        = (X \cdot \widehat{W})^2 - (X \cdot \widehat{W})^2
        = 0.
      </md>
      So every picture splits into a piece pointing along <m>W</m> and a piece
      perpendicular to <m>W</m>, and by
      <xref ref="rem-picture-pythagoras" text="type-global" /> the split is a right
      triangle:
      <md>
        \| X \|^2 = \| X_{\parallel} \|^2 + \| X_{\perp} \|^2,
        \qquad \text{where} \quad
        \| X_{\parallel} \| = | X \cdot \widehat{W} |.
      </md>
      The last equality is the scaling property again, applied to
      <m>(X \cdot \widehat{W}) \widehat{W}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      With that in hand the kernel stops being a dimension count and becomes a picture.
      By <xref ref="thm-weight-picture" text="type-global" /> the classifier's
      transformation is <m>T(G) = G \cdot W</m>, so
      <md>
        G \in \ker(T)
        \quad \text{if and only if} \quad
        G \cdot W = 0
        \quad \text{if and only if} \quad
        G \text{ is perpendicular to } W.
      </md>
      The <m>47</m>-dimensional kernel handed over by
      <xref ref="thm-rank-nullity" text="type-global" /> is precisely the set of pictures
      perpendicular to the weight picture. That is what <m>47</m> means here:
      <m>47</m> perpendicular directions of change the score cannot see at all, against
      <m>1</m> direction it sees perfectly. Rewriting the fundamental line with the split,
      <md>
        s(X + G) = s(X) + G \cdot W = s(X) + \| W \| \, (G \cdot \widehat{W}),
      </md>
      only <m>G_{\parallel}</m> ever reaches the verdict; <m>G_{\perp}</m>, however long,
      is discarded before the comparison is made.
    </p>

    <p>
      The same split measures how safe a verdict is.
      <xref ref="lem-picture-dot-bound" text="type-global" /> says that any picture
      <m>D</m> satisfies <m>| D \cdot \widehat{W} | \leq \| D \|</m>, so a picture
      <m>X</m> and any point <m>Y</m> on the boundary <m>\{ s = 0 \}</m> obey
      <md>
        \| X - Y \| \geq | (X - Y) \cdot \widehat{W} | = \frac{|s(X) - s(Y)|}{\| W \|}
        = \frac{|s(X)|}{\| W \|},
      </md>
      and the choice <m>Y = X - \frac{s(X)}{\| W \|} \widehat{W}</m>, which does lie on
      the boundary, attains it. So
      <md>
        \operatorname{dist}\left( X, \{ s = 0 \} \right) = \frac{|s(X)|}{\| W \|},
      </md>
      and the score, divided by <m>\| W \|</m>, is literally the distance from the
      picture to the decision boundary. The references sit at
      <m>7839 / 125.2 \approx 62.6</m> units from it, half the <m>125.2</m> that separate
      them<mdash /><xref ref="fig-decision-boundary" text="global" /> drawn to scale
      rather than drawn as a suggestion.
    </p>

    <p>
      Now the surprise of <xref ref="subsec-recognition-nearest" text="title" /> can be
      answered by counting directions. Grain at <m>\sigma = 25</m> moves a shot about
      <m>\sigma \sqrt{48} \approx 173</m> units, more than the <m>125.2</m> separating
      the references, and the verdicts survive anyway. They survive because those
      <m>173</m> units are spread over <m>48</m> perpendicular directions and only one of
      them is wired to the verdict. A grain picture has no relationship to <m>W</m>, so
      its length divides among the directions roughly evenly: of the <m>173</m> units,
      typically some twenty end up in <m>G_{\parallel}</m> and the remaining <m>170</m>
      or so in <m>G_{\perp}</m>, where the score never looks. The score moves by
      <m>\| W \| \, (G \cdot \widehat{W})</m><mdash />typically a couple of thousand
      against a scale of <m>7839</m><mdash />and the verdict holds.
    </p>

    <p>
      <xref ref="lem-picture-dot-bound" text="type-global" /> says what the grain is
      giving up. The largest score change a disturbance of length <m>\| G \|</m> could
      possibly cause is <m>| G \cdot W | \leq \| G \| \, \| W \|</m>, and equality
      requires <m>G</m> to be a scalar multiple of <m>W</m>. At <m>\| G \| = 173</m> that
      ceiling is <m>173 \times 125.2 \approx 21{,}700</m>, nearly three times the
      <m>7839</m> needed to flip a reference. A disturbance of exactly the size of the
      grain, aimed squarely at <m>W</m>, would demolish the machine. Grain is not aimed;
      it is scattered, and scattering is what saves the verdict.
    </p>

    <p>
      That also says, in advance, what will destroy it: a disturbance that is aimed.
      Take the all-ones picture <m>J</m> of a uniform brightening. Its length is
      <m>\| J \| = \sqrt{48} \approx 6.93</m>, and
      <md>
        J \cdot \widehat{W} = \frac{J \cdot W}{\| W \|} = \frac{688}{125.2} \approx 5.49,
      </md>
      so <m>\| J_{\parallel} \| / \| J \| \approx 5.49 / 6.93 \approx 0.79</m>: nearly
      four fifths of <m>J</m>'s length lies along <m>W</m>, where a grain picture puts
      about a tenth of its own. Almost none of an exposure change hides in the kernel.
      This is why, in <xref ref="subsec-recognition-limits" text="title" />, twelve gray
      levels of darkening<mdash />a disturbance of length
      <m>\| 12J \| \approx 83.1</m>, less than half of what the grain
      inflicted<mdash />will flip a verdict that <m>173</m> units of grain could not.
    </p>

    <p>
      The cell below dissects one grain sample and the darkening side by side. For each
      it prints the total length, the single number <m>X \cdot \widehat{W}</m> that the
      score responds to, the lengths of the two pieces, a numerical check that
      <m>\| X \|^2 = \| X_{\parallel} \|^2 + \| X_{\perp} \|^2</m>, and the change
      <m>X \cdot W</m> the disturbance would make to a score. Then it draws the grain and
      its two pieces, each shifted to mid-gray for display exactly as <m>W</m> was.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        def dotp(X, Y):
            return sum(a*b for a, b in zip(X.change_ring(RDF).list(),
                                           Y.change_ring(RDF).list()))

        normW = sqrt(dotp(W, W))
        What  = W.change_ring(RDF)/normW      # W scaled to length 1

        def split(X):
            """Split X into its part along W and its part perpendicular to W."""
            along = dotp(X, What)*What
            return along, X.change_ring(RDF) - along

        def report(name, X):
            par, perp = split(X)
            print(name)
            print("   ||X||                      =", n(sqrt(dotp(X, X)), digits=5))
            print("   X . What                   =", n(dotp(X, What), digits=5))
            print("   ||X_par||                  =", n(sqrt(dotp(par, par)), digits=5))
            print("   ||X_perp||                 =", n(sqrt(dotp(perp, perp)), digits=5))
            print("   ||X_par||^2 + ||X_perp||^2 =",
                  n(dotp(par, par) + dotp(perp, perp), digits=6),
                  "  vs  ||X||^2 =", n(dotp(X, X), digits=6))
            print("   change in score, X . W     =", n(dotp(X, W), digits=5))

        sigma = 25    # change me: the size of the grain being dissected
        G = matrix(RDF, 8, 6, [normalvariate(0, sigma) for _ in range(48)])

        report("one grain sample G", G)
        report("the darkening -12J", -12*J.change_ring(RDF))

        Gpar, Gperp = split(G)
        show_pictures(("grain G, mid-gray = 0", 128*J + G),
                      ("its part along W", 128*J + Gpar),
                      ("its part perpendicular to W", 128*J + Gperp))
      </input>
    </sage>

    <p>
      The printout for the grain shows a length of the order of <m>173</m><mdash />the
      exact figure varies from sample to sample<mdash />with a couple of dozen units of
      it along <m>\widehat{W}</m> and essentially all the rest perpendicular; the two
      squared lengths add to the total, as they must. The printout for the darkening
      shows the opposite habit: of its <m>83.1</m> units, <m>65.9</m> lie along
      <m>\widehat{W}</m> and only <m>50.6</m> perpendicular, and the score change it
      causes, <m>-8256</m>, already exceeds the <m>7839</m> that a reference has to
      spare. The pictures make the same point without numbers. The perpendicular part of
      the grain is indistinguishable from the grain itself; the part along <m>W</m> is a
      faint ghost of the weight picture, nearly flat against the mid-gray, and it is the
      only part of the disturbance the machine was ever able to see.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-recognition-average">
    <title>Learning Prototypes from Many Photographs</title>

    <p>
      Everything so far has taken the two references for granted. No recognizer in
      practice is handed the true, grainless corner to use as one. It is handed
      photographs<mdash />several of each thing, every one contaminated<mdash />and must
      build its own references out of them. The tool for combining several pictures into
      one has been in hand since <xref ref="sec-superposition" text="title" />: the
      average. Before proving anything about it, look at what it does. The cell below
      photographs the cat corner six times through deliberately brutal grain,
      <c>sigma = 35</c>, so that a single photograph is a poor memory, and puts the
      average of the six beside them.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        shots_cat  = [grainy(A0, 35) for _ in range(6)]   # change me: 35 is heavy grain
        shots_wall = [grainy(D0, 35) for _ in range(6)]
        proto_cat  = sum(shots_cat)/6     # the prototypes: averages, as in the theorem
        proto_wall = sum(shots_wall)/6

        print("distance from one shot to the true corner:", n(dist(shots_cat[0], A0), digits=4))
        print("distance from the average of six to it:   ", n(dist(proto_cat, A0), digits=4))
        show_pictures(("grainy photograph 1", shots_cat[0]),
                      ("grainy photograph 2", shots_cat[1]),
                      ("grainy photograph 3", shots_cat[2]),
                      ("average of six photographs", proto_cat),
                      ("clean reference", A0))
      </input>
    </sage>

    <p>
      The three photographs are a mess and no two are the same mess. Their average is
      visibly calmer, and the printed distances say so numerically: the average of six
      sits several times closer to the true corner than any single shot does. Grain
      pushes each pixel up or down at random, so at any given pixel the six errors
      largely cancel. A reference built this way is called a <term>prototype</term>. It
      is a picture no camera ever took<mdash />it was computed, not photographed.
    </p>

    <p>
      Averaging is not merely one way of combining the photographs. In a precise sense it
      is the best one.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-average-prototype">
      <title>The Average Is the Best Single Reference</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>X_1, \dots, X_k</m> be pictures in <m>\mathbb{M}_{mn}</m> and let
          <m>\overline{X} = \frac{1}{k}\left( X_1 + \cdots + X_k \right)</m> be their
          average. Then for every picture <m>C</m>,
          <md>
            \sum_{i=1}^{k} \| X_i - C \|^2
            = \sum_{i=1}^{k} \| X_i - \overline{X} \|^2
              + k \, \| \overline{X} - C \|^2.
          </md>
          In particular, the total squared distance from the photographs to <m>C</m> is
          smallest for exactly one picture: <m>C = \overline{X}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Write <m>X_i - C = (X_i - \overline{X}) + (\overline{X} - C)</m> and expand the
          square with the dot product:
          <md>
            \| X_i - C \|^2
            = \| X_i - \overline{X} \|^2
              + 2\, (X_i - \overline{X}) \cdot (\overline{X} - C)
              + \| \overline{X} - C \|^2.
          </md>
          Sum over <m>i</m>. The middle terms contribute
          <m>2 \left( \sum_{i=1}^{k} (X_i - \overline{X}) \right) \cdot
          (\overline{X} - C)</m>, and the definition of the average says precisely that
          <m>\sum_{i=1}^{k} (X_i - \overline{X}) = X_1 + \cdots + X_k - k\overline{X}</m>
          is the zero picture. What survives is the claimed identity, and since
          <m>k \| \overline{X} - C \|^2 \geq 0</m> with equality only at
          <m>C = \overline{X}</m>, the minimum is at the average and nowhere else.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>

    <remark xml:id="rem-recognition-residuals">
      <title>Pythagoras, Collectively</title>
      <p>
        The proof is <xref ref="rem-picture-pythagoras" text="type-global" /> in
        disguise, with one difference worth naming. There the cross term vanished because
        the two pieces were perpendicular to each other. Here the residual pictures
        <m>X_i - \overline{X}</m> need not be perpendicular to <m>\overline{X} - C</m>
        at all<mdash />no single one of them has to be. What is true is that they sum to
        the zero picture, so their cross terms cancel <em>collectively</em>, and the
        squared lengths add exactly as they would for a right triangle. The average is
        the picture at which the residuals cancel.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <p>
      The theorem settles one question and leaves another wide open.<fn>
        What it proves is that the average is the unique best single representative of
        the photographs in the <em>least-squares</em> sense: among all pictures, it and
        it alone makes the total of the squared distances to the photographs as small as
        possible. It does <em>not</em> prove that the classifier built from these
        prototypes has the highest classification accuracy obtainable from the same
        photographs. That is a different question, about verdicts rather than about
        distances, and this section does not answer it.
      </fn>
      It is also worth being plain about what the machine is given. The procedure is
      <term>supervised</term>: someone hands it photographs already labelled <em>cat</em>
      or <em>empty wall</em>, and it averages each labelled pile separately. It does not
      discover that there are two kinds of corner; it is told, and it compresses each
      told-about pile into one picture.
    </p>

    <p>
      Nothing in <xref ref="subsec-recognition-score" text="title" /> used what <m>P</m>
      and <m>Q</m> were, so the classifier carries over untouched. Where it once ran on
      <m>P = A_0</m> and <m>Q = D_0</m>, it now runs on the two prototypes, with the
      weight picture <m>W = P - Q</m> and threshold <m>\tau = M \cdot W</m> rebuilt from
      them. The cell below compares the two machines head to head: the nearest-reference
      rule using a single grainy photograph as its memory of each corner, and the same
      rule using the prototypes.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        def accuracy(refC, refW, trials=200):
            correct = 0
            for k in range(trials):
                corner = A0 if k % 2 == 0 else D0
                shot = grainy(corner, 35)
                says_cat = dist(shot, refC) &lt; dist(shot, refW)
                correct += (says_cat == (corner is A0))
            return correct/trials

        print("one grainy photograph as each reference:",
              n(accuracy(shots_cat[0], shots_wall[0]), digits=3))
        print("the average of six as each prototype:   ",
              n(accuracy(proto_cat, proto_wall), digits=3))
        show_pictures(("single-photograph reference", shots_cat[0]),
                      ("prototype: average of six", proto_cat),
                      ("clean reference", A0))
      </input>
    </sage>

    <p>
      The single-photograph machine does markedly worse than the prototype machine, which
      recovers most of what the grain destroyed. This is the first moment in the book
      where a machine can fairly be said to <em>learn</em>: shown examples, it compresses
      them, by nothing deeper than a linear combination with coefficients
      <m>\frac{1}{6}</m>, into a reference it was never given.
    </p>

    <remark xml:id="rem-recognition-average-summary">
      <title>What the Average Is Good For</title>
      <p>
        In this section the average earns its place three ways<mdash />one proved, one
        probabilistic, one demonstrated.
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              <em>It is the best one-picture summary of the pile.</em> This is what
              <xref ref="thm-average-prototype" text="type-global" /> proves: if six
              labelled photographs must be compressed into a single reference, the
              average is the unique picture minimizing the total squared distance to
              them<mdash />any other choice <m>C</m> pays the extra
              <m>k \| \overline{X} - C \|^2</m>. Nothing about verdicts; it is purely a
              statement about faithful summarization.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <em>It is a denoiser<mdash />an estimate of the clean corner the machine
              was never given.</em> Grain pushes each pixel up or down with mean zero, so
              at every pixel the <m>k</m> errors largely cancel, and the average of
              <m>k</m> shots lands on the order of <m>\sqrt{k}</m> times closer to the
              true corner than any single shot does<mdash />the improvement the first
              cell above prints.<fn xml:id="fn-recognition-root-k">
                The <m>\sqrt{k}</m> here is the additivity of variance, not the central
                limit theorem. At each pixel the <m>k</m> errors are independent with
                mean <m>0</m> and standard deviation <m>\sigma</m><mdash />the grain
                model of <xref ref="subsec-recognition-nearest" text="title" />, one
                error <m>g_{ij}</m> per pixel per shot<mdash />so their average
                <m>\overline{g}_{ij}</m> has standard deviation
                <m>\sigma/\sqrt{k}</m>, a two-line computation whose whole content is
                <m>\operatorname{Var}(\overline{g}_{ij}) = \sigma^2/k</m>. The
                <m>k \to \infty</m> version of the same fact is the law of large
                numbers. It says nothing about the <em>shape</em> of the error
                distribution; it is purely about its <em>size</em>. The central limit
                theorem, which concerns shape, appears in
                <xref ref="subsec-recognition-accuracy" text="title" />.
              </fn> The prototype is the best reconstruction of <m>A_0</m>
              available from what the machine was actually handed.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <em>Consequently it repairs the decision boundary, which is why the
              verdicts improve.</em> The classifier depends on its references only
              through <m>W = P - Q</m> and <m>\tau</m>: noisy references mean a corrupted
              <m>W</m> and a misplaced boundary, while references closer to the true
              corners mean a boundary closer to the one built from <m>A_0</m> and
              <m>D_0</m> themselves. That is what the head-to-head comparison
              demonstrates.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        The footnote to <xref ref="thm-average-prototype" text="type-global" /> lives in
        the gap between the last two items: closest to the truth in least squares
        provably gives the best summary, and in the experiment gives better verdicts, but
        it does not prove that no other reference choice could classify the same
        photographs better still. The honest slogan is that the average is the best
        memory, not provably the best judge<mdash />and a better memory happens to make a
        better judge.
      </p>
      <p>
        <xref ref="fig-recognition-mindmap" text="global" /> collects the whole story in
        one picture: how the machine decides, how its accuracy is measured, and what such
        a measurement does and does not establish.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-recognition-mindmap">
        <caption>
          From image to accuracy: the decision rule, the test procedure, and the meaning
          of the result. The starred branch is the optional one, and the branch colors
          are the book's own<mdash />olive for the rule, goldenrod for the testing,
          maroon for what the testing does and does not settle.
          <url href="external/images/recognition-mindmap.svg">Open
          the full-screen map in a new tab</url>
        </caption>
        <image source="images/recognition-mindmap.svg" width="100%">
          <shortdescription>A mind map with three branches. Branch one, how the machine decides: store the cat prototype P and wall prototype Q, prepare the decision rule with W equal to P minus Q and M equal to the midpoint of P and Q, receive a new image X, compute the score s of X as X minus M dot W, equivalent to choosing the nearer reference, and use the sign of the score: positive means cat, negative means empty wall, zero means undecided. Branch two, how accuracy is tested: use training images to build P and Q, prepare new labeled test images not used to build the prototypes, apply the same decision rule to every test image, compare each verdict with its true label as correct or incorrect, and report test accuracy as correct verdicts over total verdicts, giving both classifiers the same test set for a fair comparison; this branch is starred as optional, because it leans on a few ideas from probability that the book does not develop. Branch three, what the result establishes: it estimates accuracy under the chosen noise model and does not prove that no other classifier could do better</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-recognition-accuracy">
    <title>Assessing the Accuracy of the Verdicts*</title>

    <p>
      This subsection is starred: it leans on a few ideas from probability that this book
      does not develop, and nothing later depends on it. It answers a question the
      previous subsection deliberately left open. <xref ref="thm-average-prototype"
      text="type-global" /> proves that the average is the best representative in the
      least-squares sense; to assess the classifier's <em>verdicts</em>, we instead apply
      it to new labelled photographs that were not used to construct the prototypes. If
      it classifies <m>c</m> out of <m>N</m> such photographs correctly, then
      <md>
        \text{test accuracy} = \frac{c}{N}.
      </md>
      This answers a different question from the theorem: not how faithfully the
      prototype summarizes its pile, but how often the resulting verdict is right.
    </p>

    <p>
      For the estimate to be fair, four rules govern the experiment.
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Use labelled <em>training</em> photographs to form the cat and wall
            prototypes.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Create a separate collection of new labelled <em>test</em> photographs,
            none of which entered the prototypes.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Give exactly the same test photographs to every classifier being compared.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Count the correct verdicts and divide by the number tested.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      The head-to-head cell of the previous subsection quietly bent the third rule: it
      called <c>accuracy</c> twice, and each call manufactured its own random test set,
      so part of the printed difference is luck of the draw. The cell below repairs
      this. It builds one balanced test set and evaluates both classifiers on it. Two
      hundred trials are adequate for a demonstration; two thousand makes the estimate
      more stable.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        trials = 2000
        test_set = []
        for k in range(trials):
            true_cat = (k % 2 == 0)
            corner = A0 if true_cat else D0
            test_set.append((grainy(corner, 35), true_cat))

        def test_accuracy(ref_cat, ref_wall, data):
            correct = 0
            for shot, true_cat in data:
                says_cat = dist(shot, ref_cat) &lt; dist(shot, ref_wall)
                correct += (says_cat == true_cat)
            return correct / len(data)

        print("single-reference test accuracy:",
              n(test_accuracy(shots_cat[0], shots_wall[0], test_set), digits=4))
        print("prototype test accuracy:       ",
              n(test_accuracy(proto_cat, proto_wall, test_set), digits=4))
      </input>
    </sage>

    <p>
      The two printed numbers now differ only because the classifiers differ. Be precise
      about what they establish: the experiment estimates how accurately each
      <em>particular</em> classifier works on new photographs generated by the
      <em>chosen noise model</em>, and it can support the claim that the prototype
      classifier performs better in this experiment. It does not prove that no other
      possible classifier could do better. For real photographs the same principle
      applies<mdash />reserve labelled images that never enter the construction of the
      prototypes<mdash />and the test set should include the kinds of variation expected
      in practice, such as changes in exposure and position, not only synthetic grain.
    </p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>An Exact Calculation for Gaussian Grain</title>
      <p>
        The clean-reference classifier admits a calculation with no experiment in it at
        all. Assume the entries of the disturbance <m>G</m> are independent normal
        random variables with mean <m>0</m> and standard deviation <m>\sigma</m>, and
        temporarily ignore the clipping of gray levels at <m>0</m> and <m>255</m>. For a
        new cat photograph <m>X = A_0 + G</m>, linearity of the score gives
        <md>
          s(A_0 + G) = \tfrac{1}{2}\| W \|^2 + G \cdot W,
        </md>
        and <m>G \cdot W = \sum_{i,j} g_{ij} w_{ij}</m>, a sum of independent normal
        terms, is itself normal with mean <m>0</m> and standard deviation
        <m>\sigma \| W \|</m>. The verdict is correct exactly when the score is
        positive, so
        <md>
          P(\text{correct verdict})
          = \Phi\!\left( \frac{\| W \|}{2\sigma} \right),
        </md>
        where <m>\Phi</m> is the cumulative distribution function of a standard normal
        random variable<mdash />the probability that such a variable falls below the
        given value.<fn xml:id="fn-recognition-clt">
          The exactness of this formula leans on the assumption that the grain is
          Gaussian: a sum of independent normal terms is exactly normal. Real sensor
          grain is not Gaussian. But the score <m>G \cdot W</m> is a sum of <m>48</m>
          independent contributions, and the central limit theorem<mdash />the statement
          that such sums come out approximately normal whatever each contribution looks
          like<mdash />is what makes the formula approximately right anyway. That
          robustness, and not the Gaussian special case, is why the normal model here is
          a mild assumption rather than a fragile one.
        </fn> An empty-wall photograph gives the same probability, because the
        two classes sit symmetrically about the decision boundary.
        <xref ref="fig-recognition-score-distributions" text="global" /> draws the
        calculation: each class of shots piles its scores into a bell curve centered on
        its reference's score, and the error probability is nothing but the area of the
        tail that spills across the boundary.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-recognition-score-distributions">
        <caption>
          How the scores of grainy shots distribute themselves. Wall shots pile up
          around <m>s = -7839</m> and cat shots around <m>s = +7839</m>, each in a bell
          curve of width <m>\sigma \| W \|</m>; a shot is misjudged exactly when its
          score lands on the wrong side of the boundary, so each shaded tail
          <em>is</em> the error probability. Quadrupling the spread from
          <m>\sigma = 25</m> to <m>\sigma = 50</m> turns a negligible tail into a
          tenth of all shots.
        </caption>
        <image source="images/recognition-score-distributions.svg" width="100%">
          <shortdescription>Two panels, each showing two bell curves over the score axis: a red curve for wall shots centered at minus 7839 and a blue curve for cat shots centered at plus 7839, with a dashed vertical decision boundary at score zero. In the left panel, labeled sigma equals 25, the curves are narrow and barely overlap, and each shaded tail crossing the boundary is 0.6 percent of its curve. In the right panel, labeled sigma equals 50, the curves are twice as wide and overlap visibly, and each shaded tail is 10.5 percent of its curve</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </figure>
      <p>
        For the values of this section, <m>\| W \| = \sqrt{15678} \approx 125.2</m>
        and <m>\sigma = 25</m>, so
        <md>
          \Phi(125.2 / 50) = \Phi(2.504) \approx 0.9939:
        </md>
        the expected accuracy is about <m>99.4</m> percent, which explains the nearly
        perfect two-hundred-photograph result of
        <xref ref="subsec-recognition-nearest" text="title" />. Note also what the
        formula watches: only the ratio of the separation <m>\| W \|</m> to the grain
        <m>\sigma</m><mdash />the total displacement <m>\sigma\sqrt{48}</m> never
        appears, which is the central fact of the section restated as a probability. The
        calculation is exact for the unclipped Gaussian model with clean references.
        Once the references are themselves noisy learned prototypes, the formula becomes
        less transparent, and the held-out test experiment above is the clearer choice.
        <xref ref="fig-recognition-accuracy-curve" text="global" /> puts formula and
        experiment side by side across the whole range of grain sizes.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-recognition-accuracy-curve">
        <caption>
          The probability of a correct verdict against the grain size <m>\sigma</m>. The
          curve is the exact formula <m>\Phi(\| W \| / 2\sigma)</m>; each dot is a
          held-out test of four thousand shots at that grain size, gray levels clipped to
          <m>[0, 255]</m> as a camera would. The two agree until the grain grows so
          large that clipping<mdash />which the formula ignores<mdash />begins to
          distort the shots. Even at <m>\sigma = 100</m> the machine still beats
          guessing.
        </caption>
        <image source="images/recognition-accuracy-curve.svg" width="82%">
          <shortdescription>A graph of the probability of a correct verdict, from 0.5 to 1, against grain size sigma from 5 to 100. A smooth blue curve, labeled exact formula Phi of norm of W over two sigma, starts at 1 for small sigma and descends to about 0.73 at sigma equals 100. Orange dots, labeled simulation of 4000 shots each, follow the curve closely until about sigma equals 60 and then drift slightly below it, with an annotation explaining that clipping at 0 and 255 drags real shots below the unclipped formula. A dashed horizontal line at 0.5 is labeled guessing, and an annotation marks the section's cell at sigma equals 25 with accuracy approximately 0.994</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </paragraphs>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-recognition-limits">
    <title>What the Method Does Not See</title>

    <p>
      Return to the clean references <m>P = A_0</m> and <m>Q = D_0</m>, so that the
      numbers are the concrete ones of
      <xref ref="subsec-recognition-score" text="title" />. The line that saved the
      verdicts from grain,
      <md>
        s(X + G) = s(X) + G \cdot W,
      </md>
      predicts exactly what will <em>not</em> be survived: a disturbance that pushes
      every pixel the same way. Underexposing a patch by <m>t</m> gray levels adds
      <m>G = -tJ</m>, where <m>J</m> is the all-ones picture, and <m>J \cdot W</m> is
      simply the sum of the entries of <m>W</m><mdash />the total brightness of
      <m>A_0</m> minus the total brightness of <m>D_0</m>:
      <md>
        J \cdot W = 4210 - 3522 = 688,
      </md>
      positive because the cat corner is on the whole brighter than the bare wall.
      Nothing cancels; all <m>48</m> terms push together. The score of a darkened eye
      patch is <m>s(A_0) - 688\,t = 7839 - 688\,t</m>, which turns negative as soon as
      <m>t \gt \frac{7839}{688} \approx 11.4</m>. Darken every pixel by twelve gray
      levels out of <m>255</m><mdash />a displacement of only
      <m>\| 12 J \| \approx 83</m> units, less than half of what the grain
      inflicted<mdash />and the machine calls the cat's eye an empty wall. Turning the
      exposure down by scaling does the same: <m>s(cA_0) = 65690\,c - 57851</m> crosses
      zero at <m>c \approx 0.88</m>, so any shot at less than <m>88</m> percent of the
      original exposure is pronounced catless. The machine that shrugged off grain which
      buried the picture is undone by a hint of dusk.
    </p>

    <p>
      A second blindness costs nothing to state and everything in practice. Photograph
      the eye one pixel to the right, so that the identical content lands one column
      over, and the patch moves <m>74</m> units away from <m>A_0</m><mdash />more than
      half the distance separating a cat from no cat at all. The verdict happens to
      survive this once, but the lesson does not improve with luck: the distance
      compares pixel against pixel by position, and has no way of knowing that two
      pictures contain the same thing in different places. Try all three failures below.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        t = 12        # change me: amount of darkening, in gray levels
        c = 0.85      # change me: exposure scale
        darker = A0 - t*J
        scaled = c*A0.change_ring(RDF)
        slid   = A0.matrix_from_columns([0, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4])  # the eye, one pixel right

        for name, X in [("darkened", darker), ("scaled", scaled), ("slid", slid)]:
            verdict = "cat" if dist(X, A0) &lt; dist(X, D0) else "empty wall"
            print(name, "  distance moved:", n(dist(X, A0), digits=4),
                  "  score:", n(score(X), digits=5),
                  "  verdict:", verdict)
        show_pictures(("darkened image", darker), ("scaled image", scaled),
                      ("one-pixel-shifted image", slid), ("clean reference", A0))
      </input>
    </sage>

    <p>
      The printed table makes the point without the pictures: darkening moves the patch
      about <m>83</m> units and flips the verdict to <em>empty wall</em>; scaling moves
      it farther still and flips it too; the one-pixel shift moves it <m>74</m> units,
      more than either, and the verdict nevertheless holds at <em>cat</em>. Distance
      moved is not what decides. Only the score is.
    </p>

    <p>
      The first of those failures is worth a second look, because the repair it wants is
      already within reach. Underexposure changed the <em>length</em> of the picture
      without changing what was in it. Length is exactly what the third quantity of
      <xref ref="def-dot-product" text="type-global" /> divides out: alongside the dot
      product and the length, that definition fixes the angle between two nonzero vectors
      of <m>\R^3</m> by <m>\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v} = \| \vec{u} \| \| \vec{v} \| \cos\theta</m>.
      Once more the recipe is a formula in coordinates, so it rides to pictures.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-picture-angle">
      <title>The Angle Between Two Pictures</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For nonzero pictures <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m> in <m>\mathbb{M}_{mn}</m>, the
          <term>angle</term> between them is the unique
          <m>\theta \in [0, \pi]</m> with
          <md>
            \cos\theta = \frac{X \cdot Y}{\| X \| \, \| Y \|}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      There is exactly one such <m>\theta</m> because the fraction lies between
      <m>-1</m> and <m>1</m>, and it lies there because of
      <xref ref="lem-picture-dot-bound" text="type-global" />: the lemma proved in
      <xref ref="subsec-recognition-perpendicular" text="title" /> is precisely the
      statement that this definition is legal. Perpendicular pictures are the case
      <m>\theta = \pi/2</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Now watch what the angle does to the exposure failures. For any <m>c \gt 0</m>,
      <md>
        \cos\theta(cA_0, A_0)
        = \frac{(cA_0) \cdot A_0}{\| cA_0 \| \, \| A_0 \|}
        = \frac{c \| A_0 \|^2}{c \| A_0 \| \, \| A_0 \|}
        = 1,
      </md>
      so <m>\theta(cA_0, A_0) = 0</m>: turning the exposure up or down moves a picture
      along a ray away from the origin, changing its length and not its direction, and
      the angle never notices. The score, meanwhile, flips at <m>c \approx 0.88</m>.
      Judging by angle rather than by distance is exactly the dividing out of brightness
      that the failures have been asking for, and it needs nothing that is not already on
      this page. The angle rule reads: call <m>X</m> a cat when
      <m>\theta(X, A_0) \lt \theta(X, D_0)</m>. The cell below runs it against the
      distance rule on all three failures.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        def angle(X, Y):
            """The angle between two pictures, in degrees."""
            c = dotp(X, Y)/(sqrt(dotp(X, X))*sqrt(dotp(Y, Y)))
            c = min(1.0, max(-1.0, c))   # keep rounding out of arccos
            return n(arccos(c)*180/pi, digits=4)

        row = "%-10s %11s %11s  %-12s %11s %11s  %-12s"
        print(row % ("", "dist to A0", "dist to D0", "distance rule",
                     "angle to A0", "angle to D0", "angle rule"))
        for name, X in [("darkened", darker), ("scaled", scaled), ("slid", slid)]:
            dA, dD = dist(X, A0), dist(X, D0)
            aA, aD = angle(X, A0), angle(X, D0)
            print(row % (name,
                         n(dA, digits=4), n(dD, digits=4),
                         "cat" if dA &lt; dD else "empty wall",
                         aA, aD,
                         "cat" if aA &lt; aD else "empty wall"))
        print("for comparison, the angle between the two references:", angle(A0, D0))
      </input>
    </sage>

    <p>
      The angle rule repairs both exposure failures outright. The darkened patch sits
      <m>1.17</m> degrees from the cat corner against <m>8.286</m> degrees from the empty
      one, and the rescaled patch sits at an angle of zero from the cat corner, printed
      as zero up to rounding; both are pronounced <em>cat</em>, where the distance rule
      called them empty wall. It does nothing whatever for the third. The one-pixel shift
      was never a wrong verdict, and the angle rule returns the same right answer by the
      same thin margin<mdash /><m>6.91</m> degrees to the cat corner against
      <m>7.445</m> to the empty one. Set that beside the angle between the two references
      themselves, <m>7.294</m> degrees: the eye photographed one pixel over sits
      <m>6.91</m> degrees from the eye it actually is, while bare wall sits only
      <m>7.294</m> degrees from it. Sliding a picture one column over costs nearly as
      much angle as replacing the cat with a wall. Dividing out brightness does not teach
      a machine where things are.
    </p>

    <p>
      Each failure names the repair it is waiting for, and each repair is a course that
      exists. One of them has already started here:
      <xref ref="def-picture-angle" text="type-global" /> divides out brightness so that
      only pattern remains, and it cost nothing but the dot product. Carried through
      properly, that repair and the practice of measuring pictures along a few
      well-chosen directions instead of all <m>48</m> at once are
      the business of orthogonality, projections, and least squares<mdash />the linear
      algebra that usually follows this book. And the deepest question raised
      here<mdash />the verdict lived or died with one direction, <m>W</m>; which
      directions in a space does a matrix itself single out as the ones that
      matter?<mdash />is not a later course at all. It is the next chapter.
    </p>

    <p>
      This closes the story the cat has been carrying since
      <xref ref="sec-images-as-matrices" text="title" />. There, arithmetic acted on one
      picture; in <xref ref="sec-superposition" text="title" />, two pictures spanned a
      plane; here, pictures are compared, averaged, and finally judged. A machine that
      tells a cat from a bare wall by squaring forty-eight subtractions is not yet
      seeing. But it is deciding, its decision is a linear transformation and a
      threshold, and the linear part of a modern image recognizer still consists largely
      of the operations studied here, run at scale through billions of weights instead of
      forty-eight: weighted sums, dot products, and linear transformations. What those
      recognizers have and this one does not is the nonlinear step of
      <xref ref="rem-recognition-neuron" text="type-global" />, inserted between the
      linear stages. That step is where their additional power comes from, and it is not
      in this chapter.
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>
